spes change to the ios-flame Metal frontend (745c434 + iOSPlus10 flame/0001-0007), applied by tools/build-spes-metal-macos.sh:
a diagnostic for pictures that come out wrong (App Library, 2026-11-14: the blurred backdrop covers only the top third of the
screen, black below). Nothing changes unless /var/db/negronimetal/env sets one of:
- NEGRONIMETAL_TRACE=1: after `touch /var/db/negronimetal/trace` the next 120 command buffers (NEGRONIMETAL_TRACE_COUNT) are
  written to /var/db/negronimetal/trace.log as the client asked for them: each render pass (attachments with size, format,
  level, slice, load action asked and given, store action, renderTargetWidth/Height), pipeline function names, viewports,
  scissors, textures bound, the first floats of vertex buffers and setBytes blocks, draws, blit copies with origins and sizes,
  generateMipmaps, dispatches, new textures and views. Passes whose largest side is under NEGRONIMETAL_TRACE_MIN (64) are left out.
- NEGRONIMETAL_TRACE=3: also, after each traced encoder, 8 rows of every target (1/16 ... 15/16 of its height) are copied to a
  buffer by a blit encoder of our own on the same command buffer, and logged at completion as y<row>:<non-zero %>/<mean byte>:
  the first target whose lower rows are 0 while its sources' are not is where the picture is lost.
- NEGRONIMETAL_DONTCARE=clear|load|first|keep: what a DontCare colour attachment that is not an IOSurface loads. clear is
  flame/0002's behaviour and the default (the whole level is cleared to zero at every such pass); load keeps the texture's
  contents (tests whether a pass that redraws part of a texture loses the rest to that clear); first clears a level the first
  time a render pass targets it and loads after that (new textures still read as zero); keep hands DontCare to the runtime.
Why the DontCare knob (read in the pinned sources, not yet tried on the phone): metal2vulkan-runtime src/clear_reach.rs (720ef1ce,
lines 21-46) records Apple's measured DontCare load (M3 Max, M4): tiles a draw touches start as zero, tiles no primitive touches
keep their texels; and the runtime already does that itself, clearing only the tiles the draws' viewports and scissors reach.
flame/0002 turns every such DontCare into a Clear before the runtime sees it, so the whole level goes to zero: redundant for the
touched tiles and wrong for the rest. If CoreAnimation draws part of a texture in a DontCare pass (a band of a blur, one slot of
a cache), everything outside that part is lost. NEGRONIMETAL_DONTCARE=keep is Metal's behaviour as the runtime has it: try it first.

